  "live_records": {
    "q-333664": {
      "dom_id": "step-q333664",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t)+3 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-x(t)+2y(t)-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. The equilibrium point of the system is $[-1,\\,1]^{T}.$ Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting the equilibrium to the origin?",
      "worked_solution": "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTo shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $u=[\\begin{aligned}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,⇒\\,x=u+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x$ and ${x}^{′}$ into the given system, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}^{′} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}](u+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]u+[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]u+[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=3u(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=-u(t)+2v(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
