    "q-335386": {
      "dom_id": "step-q335386",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+6y(t)-12 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)+2x(t)+y(t)-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. An equilibrium point of the system is $[1,\\,3]^{T}.$ Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting this equilibrium to the origin?",
      "worked_solution": "To shift the system’s equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}],\\,u=[\\begin{aligned}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\,⇒\\,x=u+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nIn coordinate form, this can be written as follows:\n\n$$\nx(t)=u(t)+1,\\,y(t)=v(t)+3,\\,{x}^{′}(t)={u}^{′}(t),\\,{v}^{′}(t)={y}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now substitute these into the given system.\n\n- Substituting into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =3x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+6y(t)-12 \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =3(u(t)+1)-(v(t)+3)^{2}+6(v(t)+3)-12 \\\\  & =3u(t)+3-({v}^{2}(t)+6v(t)+9)+6v(t)+18-12 \\\\  & =3u(t)-{v}^{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & ={x}^{2}(t)+2x(t)+y(t)-6 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) & =(u(t)+1)^{2}+2(u(t)+1)+(v(t)+3)-6 \\\\  & =({u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+1)+2u(t)+2+v(t)+3-6 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)+4u(t)+v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=3u(t)-{v}^{2}(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+4u(t)+v(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
